# Copy to .env and fill in from your Supabase project (Settings → API).
# ONLY PUBLISHABLE keys belong client-side — keys designed to sit in a bundle
# any user can unzip. Row Level Security is what protects the data.
#
# The AI provider key is NOT here and must NEVER be: it lives only in the edge
# function environment →  supabase secrets set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
#
# RevenueCat's SECRET key (sk_…) is likewise a server credential and must never
# appear here. Only the public iOS SDK key (appl_…) does.
#
# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE AND IS TRACKED IN GIT. It carried the real project URL
# and a real anon JWT until 10 Sep 2026 (S9) — publishable values, but a tracked
# file naming the production project is how a development machine and a user's
# data end up pointed at the same database. Fill these in your own untracked
# `.env`; leave the placeholders here.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<your-project>.supabase.co
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<your-anon-key>

# RevenueCat → Project settings → API keys → "Public app-specific API key".
#
# TWO PREFIXES, AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE:
#   appl_…  the iOS App Store key. The only one a release may ship with.
#   test_…  a RevenueCat Test Store key. Purchases are SIMULATED — no App Store,
#           no money, no receipt. Fine for development against the dashboard's
#           offerings; `env.ts` REFUSES it in a non-__DEV__ bundle so a test key
#           can never put a fake price or a free entitlement in front of a payer
#           (CLAUDE.md §2 rule 5).
#
# Leaving it empty is a WORKING STATE: the paywall then says it cannot reach the
# App Store and shows no price, rather than inventing one. See SECURITY.md step
# 8 for the full billing setup.
EXPO_PUBLIC_REVENUECAT_IOS_KEY=

# Sentry → Project settings → Client Keys (DSN). A DSN can only WRITE events
# into one project, which is why it may sit in a bundle; the auth token that
# uploads source maps is a server credential and belongs in EAS, never here.
# Empty is a WORKING STATE and the default: `lib/crash.ts` then initialises
# nothing, sends nothing and opens no socket. What a report carries when this
# IS set is stated in the privacy policy (`lib/legal.ts` → "If Recore crashes").
EXPO_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# THE DEVELOPMENT SIGN-IN DOOR (src/lib/auth/dev-sign-in.ts), and it is OPTIONAL.
# Empty is the default and a working state: the sign-in row then explains what to
# set instead of opening anything.
#
# Both values reach the client bundle — that is what EXPO_PUBLIC_ means. Set them
# only in your own untracked .env, only against a DEVELOPMENT project, and never
# for a build you hand to anyone. The account must already exist; the door signs
# in and can no longer create one (S9/S3, 10 Sep 2026).
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_EMAIL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_PASSWORD=

# COACH MODE — the coach ↔ client layer, OFF unless a build asks for it.
#
# Build-time only (`lib/env.ts` → `isCoachModeOn`), never a stored preference:
# a release must be able to ship with the feature provably absent, and Metro
# inlines EXPO_PUBLIC_ values so the branches become dead code the bundler can
# see. `eas.json` sets it in no build profile, so cloud builds ship it OFF.
#
# WHAT TURNING IT ON MEANS, beyond the screens. A linked coach can read the text
# of your sessions, the sets read out of them, your session rating and your
# check-in notes; a comment's text is handed to Expo's push service to reach the
# phone. The privacy policy says so — and says so ONLY when this is set, because
# `lib/legal.ts` gates that section on this same flag. Do not route around that
# gate: shipping the feature with a policy that does not mention it is the one
# way this flag can cause real harm.
EXPO_PUBLIC_COACH_MODE=

# BETA UNLOCK — a build handed to a tester, with no store behind it.
#
# Build-time only (`lib/env.ts` → `isBetaUnlocked`), like coach mode. It is set
# by ONE place, the `testflight` profile in `eas.json`, and belongs in no `.env`:
# development already resolves to entitled when no RevenueCat key is configured,
# so setting it locally would only hide which path you are actually testing.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES: the dispatcher sends a signed-out person to sign-in instead
# of the paywall, the entitlement resolves to entitled, and the You tab's
# subscription group becomes one row reading "Beta · billing off". WHAT IT MUST
# NEVER DO is appear in the `production` profile — that build goes on sale, and
# this flag would give it away for free while printing no price to anyone.
EXPO_PUBLIC_BETA_UNLOCK=
